WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:03.090 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
What makes the same digit worth ten times
as much?

2
00:00:03.370 --> 00:00:06.980 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
Its position does. In our base ten system,
ten ones

3
00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:09.630 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
make one ten, and ten tens make one
hundred.

4
00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:12.920 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
Each place is ten times the place
immediately to its

5
00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:16.490 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
right. Look at four hundred and seventy
two thousand, six

6
00:00:16.490 --> 00:00:17.520 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
hundred and thirty one.

7
00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:21.120 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
Put each digit into its own column, from
hundred thousands

8
00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:23.930 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
down to ones. The seven is in the ten
thousands

9
00:00:23.930 --> 00:00:27.860 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
column. Its value is seventy thousand, not
seven thousand.

10
00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:31.140 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
The two stands for two thousand, the six
for six

11
00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:32.850 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
hundred, and the three for thirty.

12
00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:35.860 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
Reading the number in groups helps: four
hundred and seventy

13
00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:38.190 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
two thousand, then six hundred and thirty
one.

14
00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:40.180 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
Every digit has a job.

15
00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:42.940 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
Now compare the two fives in fifty five
thousand.

16
00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:45.330 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
The first five represents fifty thousand.

17
00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:47.240 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
The second represents five thousand.

18
00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:51.050 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
Fifty thousand is ten times five thousand,
even though the

19
00:00:51.050 --> 00:00:52.230 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
digits look the same.

20
00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:56.220 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
This relationship helps us multiply and
divide whole numbers by

21
00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:59.680 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
ten. Thirty four times ten is three
hundred and forty.

22
00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:02.750 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
Each digit takes a place worth ten times
as much.

23
00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:06.400 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
Three hundred and forty divided by ten
returns thirty four.

24
00:01:06.870 --> 00:01:10.420 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
Try the page example: five hundred and
eighty three thousand,

25
00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:11.950 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
two hundred and fourteen.

26
00:01:12.290 --> 00:01:14.260 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
The eight is in the ten thousands place,
so it

27
00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:19.110 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
represents eighty thousand. Remember: name
the place first, then work

28
00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:21.800 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
out the value. One place left means ten
times as

29
00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:22.180 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
much.

